From a756e309085c2cf1c82401b845fb114bc3e38ba8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 12:50:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] microblaze: Use the generic unwinder-based backtrace

The MicroBlaze backtrace walks the stack by scanning backwards from
each return address for an "addik r1,r1,-N" instruction and treating
that as the frame allocation.  That misfires on any function whose
prologue is not of that exact shape, stops after a handful of frames,
and reports __backtrace itself as the first entry, so
debug/tst-backtrace2 and tst-backtrace3 fail without any signal
involved.  It also has its own copy of the kernel signal trampoline
matching for the signal-frame case, which belongs in libgcc.

GCC emits DWARF unwind information for MicroBlaze, so drop the
special version and use debug/backtrace.c, which walks with
_Unwind_Backtrace like every other port.  Unwinding through a signal
frame additionally needs libgcc's MD_FALLBACK_FRAME_STATE_FOR for
MicroBlaze (libgcc/config/microblaze/linux-unwind.h in GCC master),
which is what tst-backtrace4 through 6 exercise.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sysdeps/microblaze/Makefile                 |   4 -
 sysdeps/microblaze/backtrace.c              | 143 --------------------
 sysdeps/microblaze/backtrace_linux.c        |  59 --------
 sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/microblaze/Makefile |   4 -
 4 files changed, 210 deletions(-)
 delete mode 100644 sysdeps/microblaze/backtrace.c
 delete mode 100644 sysdeps/microblaze/backtrace_linux.c

diff --git a/sysdeps/microblaze/Makefile b/sysdeps/microblaze/Makefile
index f7c95ae115..6ca8d02fed 100644
--- a/sysdeps/microblaze/Makefile
+++ b/sysdeps/microblaze/Makefile
@@ -5,7 +5,3 @@ endif
 ifeq ($(subdir),gmon)
 sysdep_routines += _mcount
 endif
-
-ifeq ($(subdir),resource)
-sysdep_routines += backtrace_linux
-endif
diff --git a/sysdeps/microblaze/backtrace.c b/sysdeps/microblaze/backtrace.c
deleted file mode 100644
index 54c8a6482a..0000000000
--- a/sysdeps/microblaze/backtrace.c
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,143 +0,0 @@
